/**
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *  GENE KEYS — EQ  (position-specific readings)
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *  Mars at birth: the feeling one. How emotion actually moves through you —
 *  what triggers it, how fast it arrives, what it takes to complete.
 *
 *  Schema: wiring, root, defaultMode, blindSpot, underusedStrength.
 *  wiring            — the flat behavioral fact: how feeling moves in you.
 *  root              — where this grammar of feeling likely formed.
 *  defaultMode       — the automatic move under emotional pressure.
 *  blindSpot         — what you can't see about your own emotional pattern.
 *  underusedStrength — the real advantage this wiring gives you, unused.
 *
 *  Written through the EQ lens alone — the grammar of feeling itself, not
 *  the body (Radiance), not career (Life's Work), not relationship chemistry
 *  (Attraction). Never a reworiding of the old spiritual gift/shadow/siddhi
 *  language; freshly written in the psychological register.
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 */

DGKRoles.register('eq', {
  "1": {
    "wiring": "Feeling arrives in you as something new each time, not a rerun of the last version. When a feeling has gone stale, you notice the exact moment it curdles and you stop performing it. You do not reach for a familiar comfort script when someone else is hurting; the response gets built fresh, on the spot.",
    "root": "Repetition got tagged early as dishonesty — a feeling that kept its old shape past its use-by date read as performance, not truth. Staying responsive to what's actually happening became worth more than staying consistent. Freshness became the standard you trust, and consistency became suspect.",
    "defaultMode": "When your own aliveness runs dry, you go flat and stay flat rather than fake the next response. Someone brings you something real and you register almost nothing back, then either force a reply or disappear and call it needing space. The other person is left unsure whether they've become a burden.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how often the flatness reads as rejection rather than depletion — people can't tell the difference between you being empty and you having checked out on them. You assume your silence explains itself. It doesn't; it just looks like withdrawal.",
    "underusedStrength": "Because nothing in you is rehearsed, your response to someone actually fits what they're going through right now instead of what worked last time. That specificity is rare and it's the reason people trust what you say to them. Most comfort is generic; yours isn't."
  },
  "2": {
    "wiring": "You locate the true center of a difficult emotional moment before anyone has named where it's actually coming from. In a room where grief or anger has no clear source, you turn toward it directly instead of turning away. Other people orient off your orientation without realizing that's what they're doing.",
    "root": "Being the one who could locate the feeling in a room got rewarded early, before there was language for what that even was. Not knowing where a feeling was coming from felt like being lost inside somebody else's life. So locating direction became the thing you trust more than anything else you carry.",
    "defaultMode": "When someone's emotion fills the space, your own sense of where you stand disappears, and you either mirror them completely or freeze at a distance neither of you can explain afterward. You attach hard to whichever feeling is loudest nearby. Afterward you can describe exactly what they felt and nothing about what you felt.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't register that your orientation depends on there being a strong feeling to orient around — without one, you feel genuinely lost, and nobody watching you would guess that, because you look composed the whole time. The confusion is invisible from the outside. Only you know the compass has stopped working.",
    "underusedStrength": "Your read on where a feeling is actually coming from settles a room faster than any advice offered in it. Most people manage distress by facing away from it; you face toward it, and that alone changes what happens next. It's a rarer skill than you treat it as."
  },
  "3": {
    "wiring": "You let a messy, contradictory feeling stay disordered until its own shape shows up, instead of tidying it into something repeatable. Chaotic people settle down around you because you don't need the chaos to resolve before you can be near it. Most people need distress to make sense before they can sit with it; you don't carry that requirement.",
    "root": "Order got associated early with cutting something off before it was finished — choosing one clean version of a feeling meant losing the rest of it. So staying in the mess became more honest than resolving it. Tidiness started to look like a kind of lie.",
    "defaultMode": "Small emotional demands land as overwhelm, and instead of one clear response you produce five half-responses, all sincere and none finished. The other person leaves more confused than when they arrived. You escalate rather than land, adding detail until the original hurt gets lost in it.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see that what feels like thoroughness to you reads as evasion to the person waiting for an actual answer. They experience your complexity as you avoiding commitment, not as honesty. You think you're being accurate; they think you're stalling.",
    "underusedStrength": "Your tolerance for unresolved feeling means people can bring you something genuinely messy without it needing to be pre-sorted first. That's rare — most people require a tidy story before they'll engage. You let the raw version stay raw long enough for something true to surface in it."
  },
  "4": {
    "wiring": "You understand why someone lashed out at you almost as fast as they did it, mid-argument rather than days later. Warmth toward a person returns quickly once the reason behind their behavior becomes visible to you. Most people need an apology and several days before they get there; you arrive while the exchange is still happening.",
    "root": "Fast understanding got you out of prolonged conflict early — staying angry past the point of comprehension felt pointless once the reason was already visible. So speed of understanding became the skill you lean on. Slow forgiveness started to look like a choice rather than a necessity.",
    "defaultMode": "Someone repeats a mistake that's already been explained, and warmth drops out within a second, replaced by a clipped verdict about exactly what's wrong with them. The case gets closed quietly, without them being told. Every later exchange gets colored by a decision they never knew was made.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice when a person has been filed under 'already understood' and therefore stopped being really listened to. To you it feels like efficiency; to them it feels like being written off before they finished speaking. The closed file is invisible from your side of it.",
    "underusedStrength": "Your speed at understanding someone's reasons, even mid-conflict, changes what an argument is able to become while it's still happening. Most people need the argument to end before that kind of clarity arrives. Yours arrives inside it, which is what lets people say the unfinished thing to you that they've never said to anyone else."
  },
  "5": {
    "wiring": "Your patience with someone else's slow feeling doesn't have a clock running inside it. When someone circles the same grief for the fourth time in a month, your attention doesn't thin out or start quietly planning ahead. Most people's patience is a countdown; yours isn't.",
    "root": "An unhurried pace got treated early as the only honest one — rushing a feeling to completion felt like faking that it was finished. So waiting became trustworthy, and urgency became suspect. The rhythm you extend to others is the rhythm nobody extended to you fast enough, so you learned to supply it yourself.",
    "defaultMode": "A conversation that won't get to the point turns you brittle, and you start finishing sentences, offering fixes, moving someone along a schedule they never agreed to. Endings get rushed. Your own grief then gets the same treatment and goes underground instead of through.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the moment your patience runs out, because it looks from the outside like the same steady presence you always offer — until the rushing starts, and people can't tell it apart from your ordinary care. The shift is invisible to you from the inside. It isn't to them.",
    "underusedStrength": "Letting a feeling take exactly the time it needs, without pressuring it toward completion, is why the slow ones eventually open up to you. Nobody else in their life offered that rhythm. It's a specific, transferable skill, not just a personality trait."
  },
  "6": {
    "wiring": "Your voice drops instead of rising when two people are escalating in front of you. You stay inside the friction and keep talking rather than hitting back or leaving the room. The exchange loses several degrees within seconds of your presence entering it.",
    "root": "Staying in a fight rather than fleeing it got rewarded as the version of you that kept things from breaking. Leaving felt like it would cost more than staying and absorbing the heat. So tolerance for friction became the skill, and avoidance became the thing you distrust in yourself.",
    "defaultMode": "A raised voice cuts straight through you and you argue to win, saying the accurate cruel thing that can't be unsaid afterward. Or the opposite happens: the exchange gets avoided entirely, agreement given to keep things pleasant, resentment carried for months under the name of being reasonable. Either way the real disagreement stays unspoken.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see that keeping the peace and actually resolving something are two different acts, and you frequently mistake the first for the second. The relationship looks calm to you because the fight didn't happen. It's not calm; it's postponed.",
    "underusedStrength": "Staying inside friction without needing to win it is a nervous-system skill very few people can sustain. It's why people want you in the room when something hard has to be said. Your steadiness under someone's anger is a real capacity, not a personality trait you happened to end up with."
  },
  "7": {
    "wiring": "You become the person a group checks for a reaction before deciding how they're going to feel about a situation. When a group's feeling fractures into panic or blame, that responsibility doesn't spike your own alarm the way it does most people's. One ordinary sentence from you in a bad hour keeps several other people from coming apart.",
    "root": "Steadiness under a group's fear became your position early — someone had to hold the temperature, and it fell to you before you had any training for the job. Being unshaken became the role you occupy without choosing it each time. The alternative, letting the group's panic land on you too, never felt survivable.",
    "defaultMode": "Under pressure, the people around you get sorted into those who are with you and those who aren't, and your warmth follows that line without a conscious decision being made. Loyalty gets secured through hinting and quiet alliance-building rather than open trust. You speak for other people's feelings without asking first, and they feel used by the very person protecting them.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the moment loyalty-securing replaces honest listening — from where you sit it still feels like protecting the group. They experience it as being managed. That gap is the part you can't see from inside your own good intentions.",
    "underusedStrength": "Your calm functions as a genuine stabilizer in a group under pressure, not a performance of calm. People follow your lead not because you sound certain but because you're actually steady. That's a resource most groups in crisis don't have access to at all."
  },
  "8": {
    "wiring": "What moves you shows up in a form that's unmistakably yours rather than the generic version everyone else in the room performs. You don't flatten your reaction to fit the company you're in. People recognize your emotional signature the way they'd recognize a voice through a wall.",
    "root": "Being specific about what actually moved you got treated somewhere along the line as either too much or not enough, and the safety of a borrowed register looked easier than exposure. Keeping your true response is the thing you fought to protect. Fitting in quietly by matching the room's tone is the thing that felt like erasure.",
    "defaultMode": "In a room that seems unimpressed, the real response gets swapped for a safe one, and you go home flat and faintly ashamed without being able to say why. A cooler, funnier, more measured register gets borrowed instead of the true one. The people who needed the true thing get a performance instead.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how often people around you have only ever met your imitation of yourself, not the real register underneath it. From inside, the edited version still feels like you. From outside, it reads as slightly absent.",
    "underusedStrength": "Your particular, unedited way of feeling things is exactly what makes your comfort land instead of sounding like a printed card. Most people flatten their reactions to fit in; you don't, and that gives everyone else permission to be specific too. It's the reason your warmth actually reaches people."
  },
  "9": {
    "wiring": "You keep showing up in small ways long after the emotional payoff has dropped to nothing. Support from you is a rate, not a burst — repeated attention across months rather than one dramatic gesture. Most support fades inside a fortnight; yours doesn't.",
    "root": "Big gestures got associated early with grand promises that didn't hold up, while small repeated ones quietly did. So the small became trustworthy and the large became suspect. Consistency over time became the currency you actually believe in.",
    "defaultMode": "A difficult feeling gets parked with a promise to deal with it properly once there's space, and then months pass with the same weight sitting in the same corner. You wait for a bigger gesture that never quite gets made. With other people it shows up as slow replies to real distress, arriving after the moment has closed.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how a backlog of small unaddressed things starts to look, from outside, exactly like indifference — even though internally you know you were always planning to get to it. The delay is invisible to you as a pattern. To the other person, it's the whole relationship.",
    "underusedStrength": "The rate at which you show up, unglamorous and repeated, is what actually carries people through a long stretch of grief. It feels like doing very little from where you sit. Across months, it becomes the part they remember."
  },
  "10": {
    "wiring": "You answer at your own natural size when feeling floods a room, without inflating or shrinking to match it. Concern isn't performed for the room; you meet the actual weight of a situation and stay recognizably yourself while doing it. That proportion is itself what settles other people.",
    "root": "Staying the same size regardless of the room's temperature got established early as safer than amplifying to match it — matching felt like losing your own footing. So consistency of self became the anchor. Performing a bigger or smaller reaction than you actually had started to feel dishonest.",
    "defaultMode": "Another person's distress becomes, within seconds, a question about you — how you handled it, how you appeared, whether you're the sort of person who copes with these things. Your own story gets inserted into their crisis, or you fall silent and monitor yourself instead of listening. The person in front of you can tell they're being watched from inside your self-assessment.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the moment attention shifts from them to your own performance of coping — it feels, internally, like still being present. It isn't. The loop runs quietly enough that you rarely catch it happening.",
    "underusedStrength": "Staying recognizably yourself in an emotionally charged moment gives other people something stable to stand on. That ordinariness is rarer than it's ever looked to you from the inside of your own life. It's a genuine resource, not a lack of drama."
  },
  "11": {
    "wiring": "You keep a clear picture of who someone is underneath a heavy mood and speak to that person rather than to the mood on top. When someone's despairing, encouragement from you reminds them what they're actually like rather than asking them to just feel better. That's a different and more durable intervention than most comfort offers.",
    "root": "Holding an accurate, generous picture of someone got built as the way you kept caring for people through their worst stretches. Seeing only the mood in front of you felt like abandoning who they actually were. So the picture became the thing you protect, sometimes more carefully than the actual relationship in front of you.",
    "defaultMode": "What you love is the version held in your head, and the actual person — irritable and ordinary on a Tuesday — starts to feel like a betrayal of it. You drift away, or push them toward the vision with a pressure that reads to them as disappointment. They end up feeling measured rather than loved.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how the picture you carry of someone can become a standard they're quietly failing to meet. From your side it still feels like devotion. From theirs, it can feel like being loved for a version of themselves that doesn't exist yet.",
    "underusedStrength": "Reminding someone what they're actually like, rather than just soothing the mood on top, is a rarer and more useful form of care than most encouragement offers. Almost nobody carries that kind of picture of the people they love. It's why people come to you when their own self-image goes dark."
  },
  "12": {
    "wiring": "You notice the seam when someone offers a rehearsed version of their grief, and you wait without exposing them until the true thing underneath comes up. Most people take emotional statements at face value; you register the gap and keep the door open instead. The honest disclosure eventually arrives because of that restraint.",
    "root": "Detecting the difference between a genuine feeling and a performed one got sharpened early because performed feeling once cost something to trust. So accuracy about what's real became protective. Restraint about calling it out immediately became the way you kept people from shutting down.",
    "defaultMode": "Someone reaches toward you and you become aware of how your response looks — the elegant sentence, the correct measure of emotion — while the actual person slides out of focus. Or you withhold entirely, deciding their feeling is unworthy of a real answer from you. People at their least presentable reach for you at the exact hour you're hardest to reach.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how often your discernment about what's genuine tips into judging the person for not being tidier about their pain. It feels like taste to you. It reads to them as being assessed rather than met.",
    "underusedStrength": "Your combination of accuracy and restraint — noticing the seam without exposing it — is why people trust you with material they've never rehearsed for anyone else. That pairing is unusual. Most people either take things at face value or call out the performance too bluntly to be trusted with more."
  },
  "13": {
    "wiring": "You keep receiving what someone is saying well past the point where most listeners would have started formulating a reply. The thing underneath their words reaches you intact, and you don't rush to prove that it did. Strangers tell you things because of this, without you doing anything to solicit it.",
    "root": "Listening became the currency of trust early because being fully heard once mattered more than being advised. So receiving without immediately converting it into advice became the skill you built. Interrupting with a solution started to feel like a small betrayal of what was actually being offered to you.",
    "defaultMode": "The room's grief gets taken on unfiltered, and then you find yourself irritable with the very people who confided in you, unable to explain why. Secrets accumulate, sides multiply, and you become the hinge between people who are no longer speaking to each other. Your own weather gets so crowded with other people's that you can't locate a feeling that began in you.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how much you've taken on until the irritability shows up somewhere unrelated — the connection between listening too much and snapping at someone small stays invisible to you in the moment. From outside it looks like moodiness. It's actually overload.",
    "underusedStrength": "Taking in another person's feeling without immediately converting it into advice repairs things you'll never find out about. That capacity is rare — most listeners are already composing their reply. Yours stays receptive well past that point."
  },
  "14": {
    "wiring": "You give from the middle of your own supply, not from whatever's left over at the end of the day. When someone's depleted, your response is generous and practical, and it replenishes you rather than draining you. Tired people gravitate toward you without knowing exactly why.",
    "root": "Giving from abundance rather than scraps got established early as the version of generosity that actually felt sustainable to you. Rationing warmth felt like a betrayal of what you actually had to offer. So the habit of giving freely, rather than carefully, became the one you trust.",
    "defaultMode": "An arrangement gets accepted that quietly costs you — less honesty, less contact, a thinner version of what you actually wanted — because asking for more felt greedy in the moment. Months on, the warmth has gone thin and you're performing a generosity you no longer feel. You resent people who never knew a negotiation had happened.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't register the exact moment your own wanting got overridden by the fear that wanting more would look like a character flaw. It doesn't feel like a decision at the time — it feels like being reasonable. Only the resentment later reveals that something was actually given up.",
    "underusedStrength": "What you give costs you nothing to give, because the sense of a finite supply that most people ration by never actually operates in you. Generosity here is simply what happens when nothing is being withheld. That's a genuinely rare emotional economy."
  },
  "15": {
    "wiring": "You warm to people across an enormous range — the obviously impressive and the entirely unremarkable — without giving either a different reception. When someone grieves in an inconvenient or unattractive way, your attention doesn't withdraw the way most people's does. That tolerance is a real capacity, not a virtue you decided to adopt.",
    "root": "Withdrawing attention from someone's unattractive grief got read early as a kind of cruelty you didn't want to be capable of. So staying present regardless of how someone's feeling looks became the standard you hold yourself to. It's why people who've been written off elsewhere bloom around you.",
    "defaultMode": "Somebody's excitement or pain lands on a flat surface with no give in it, and you go through the motions of caring while privately waiting for the season to change. You press people who run to extremes, needing their intensity as proof that you still work properly, then blame them for being too much. The closest ones start protecting themselves from your winters.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the flat stretch coming, or how it reads to the people around you as unreliability rather than as weather passing through. From inside, it feels temporary and forgivable. From outside, it's become a pattern they've learned to brace for.",
    "underusedStrength": "Your tolerance for the whole spectrum of human feeling — the raw, the unfinished, the grieving — is a genuine capacity most people don't have. It's why people who've been written off elsewhere settle in your company. You were simply the first one who didn't flinch."
  },
  "16": {
    "wiring": "You follow a feeling through several registers in one conversation — grief into anger into laughter — without losing the thread or getting thrown off by any of them. Most people meet a single emotional key well and go stiff in every other one. Your range means nobody has to choose which feeling to bring you.",
    "root": "Flexibility across registers got built as the way you kept a relationship intact when the emotional weather shifted fast. Rigid response to one register only would have cost you contact with people whose feelings moved quickly. So range became a skill you built early, without realizing you were building it.",
    "defaultMode": "Real feeling gets offered, it lands wrong, and something closes so completely that a week later you can look at the same person with nothing whatsoever in you. The withdrawal is instant and total rather than risking a second disappointment. It reads to them as a light going out with no explanation.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how total the shutdown looks from outside — to you it feels like protecting yourself from a repeat. To them it's unexplained abandonment. The gap between your internal logic and their experience of it stays hidden from you.",
    "underusedStrength": "Following a hard emotional exchange through several shifts without getting stiff in any of them is a skill, not luck. It's why difficult conversations actually go somewhere with you rather than stalling at the first register. Nobody has to manage which feeling they're allowed to bring you."
  },
  "17": {
    "wiring": "You notice which way someone's agitation will break — toward tears, blame, or a long silence — before their own face has caught up with it. Most people respond to the emotion directly in front of them; you respond to the one arriving in ten minutes. That anticipation happens too fast in you to feel like thinking.",
    "root": "Reading ahead of the moment got built as protection because being caught off guard by someone's shift once cost something. So forecasting became the safer position than reacting. Certainty about what's coming next replaced the discomfort of not knowing.",
    "defaultMode": "Somebody begins describing a hurt and the diagnosis gets delivered before they've finished, accurate and unwanted, received as being overruled in their own story. Certainty arrives early and then gets defended hard. You correct people about their own feelings, which is the fastest way to lose them.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't register how often your accurate forecast lands as a verdict rather than an offer. It feels like helpfulness from where you sit. To them, it feels like being told what they feel before they've said it.",
    "underusedStrength": "Anticipating which way a situation will break, before it breaks, is why things de-escalate around you so reliably. It isn't a party trick — it's genuine foresight that happens too fast to notice as thinking. Most people only respond to what's already visible; you're already ahead of it."
  },
  "18": {
    "wiring": "You name what's off in an emotional situation with enough care that the other person can admit it instead of defending themselves. When someone says they're fine and plainly aren't, you catch the discrepancy and manage the timing of naming it at once. Most people either miss it entirely or point at it clumsily.",
    "root": "Naming a discrepancy carefully rather than bluntly got built as the way you got to fix things early instead of watching them fester. Silence about what was obviously wrong felt like complicity. So the pairing of accuracy and timing became something you practiced without calling it a skill.",
    "defaultMode": "A small emotional failing in someone you love catches your eye, and returning to it becomes compulsive — correcting, mentioning, filing it away as evidence for later. Affection gets audited, and the people being audited become careful with you rather than close to you. Turned inward, the same eye goes over your own reactions until nothing you feel seems acceptable to show.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the audit happening as it happens — it feels like caring closely, not like scrutiny. The people closest to you can tell they're being tracked. You genuinely can't feel the difference from inside it.",
    "underusedStrength": "Managing both accuracy and timing when naming what's off in a situation is a rare pairing. It's why people around you get to address things early instead of long after the damage is done. Most people only manage one half of that combination."
  },
  "19": {
    "wiring": "You register unspoken need before the person carrying it has admitted to themselves that they have it. When someone walks in holding something heavy, your whole system adjusts the room around them without any announcement. Most people wait to be told; your reading arrives before the request does.",
    "root": "Sensing need early became the way you stayed necessary to people, before you had another way to secure closeness. Waiting to be asked felt riskier than anticipating. So the sensitivity became a form of information you trust more than what people actually tell you out loud.",
    "defaultMode": "Another person's mood becomes your weather, and rest is impossible while they remain unsettled, so managing them gets carried out under the name of loving them. You make yourself indispensable, then resent the dependency you built, then feel guilty about the resentment. Neither of you ever learns whether they'd want you without the management.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the exact point where attunement turns into control — from your side it still reads as care the whole way through. The other person experiences a slow narrowing of their own independence. You can't feel it happening because it wears the same face as love.",
    "underusedStrength": "Sensing unspoken need is information, not anxiety, and the people nearest you rely on it far more than they say aloud. Most people wait to be told what's needed; your reading arrives before the request does. That's a rare, practical form of attentiveness."
  },
  "20": {
    "wiring": "Your attention is undivided when someone's falling apart, and they can feel that before you've said a single word. Nothing of you is left behind in the room you just walked out of. Most comfort is delivered by people who are half elsewhere; yours isn't.",
    "root": "Full presence got built as the thing that actually helped when nothing else did — divided attention once registered as no help at all. So undivided attention became the standard you hold for yourself in a crisis. Skimming a moment started to feel like a small failure of care.",
    "defaultMode": "The right words come out at the right volume, none of it costing anything, and the other person leaves technically comforted and privately alone with the whole thing. Exchanges get skimmed and kept moving; frequency of contact gets mistaken for depth of it. A wide circle of people who like you accumulates, and none who would call at three in the morning.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the difference between skimming and fully landing until much later — in the moment, the skim feels adequate. The other person can tell immediately. That gap stays invisible to you because the words sounded right.",
    "underusedStrength": "Full contact with someone's worst hour, without half of you being elsewhere, is the single most valuable thing you bring to it. That kind of attention remains genuinely rare. You treat it as ordinary; it isn't."
  },
  "21": {
    "wiring": "You take the weight and make the decision when a situation turns frightening, and everybody present breathes out at roughly the same moment. Responsibility gets taken without anyone being made to feel small for having needed it. Your courage arrives during the danger, not after it's passed.",
    "root": "Taking charge in a frightening moment got built early as the thing that kept a situation from getting worse — waiting for someone else to act felt like a risk you couldn't afford. So responsibility became a reflex rather than a choice. Letting go of control started to feel like inviting something you wouldn't recover from.",
    "defaultMode": "Somebody's grief becomes a thing to be handled, with timelines set and decisions made on their behalf, and your care starts sounding like instruction. You take charge of moments that only wanted your company. Nobody ever gets to look after you, because you've never once been in the room without a job to do.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the moment care turns into command — from inside it still feels like helping. The people you're helping experience being managed rather than accompanied. That distinction is invisible to you until someone names it directly.",
    "underusedStrength": "Taking responsibility during real danger, rather than after it, is a nervous-system difference most people don't have access to. It's why people who never ask anyone for anything end up asking you anyway. That's a genuine strength, not just a habit of control."
  },
  "22": {
    "wiring": "Your courtesy holds without turning cold when somebody's furious at you directly. Most people match whatever register they're handed; you offer something better instead. That composure changes what the other person is able to say next.",
    "root": "Composure under attack got built as the way an exchange got a chance to become a real conversation instead of a war. Matching someone's anger felt like it would close the door permanently. So staying courteous under pressure became the skill you rely on more than winning the argument.",
    "defaultMode": "A moment overwhelms you and the behavior that follows doesn't match who you actually are — cutting or seductive or wildly disproportionate — and then months go into managing the memory of it. You either over-control until you're charming and unreachable, or spill everywhere at once and withdraw in embarrassment. The middle range, where feeling can be strong and still dignified, goes missing.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how rarely people get access to your actual intensity, because you've spent so long managing whether it looks graceful. To you the control feels like maturity. To the people who want real closeness, it can feel like never quite being let in.",
    "underusedStrength": "Keeping your manners when the emotional temperature spikes lets another person climb down from their anger without being humiliated. That's not politeness — it's a specific competence. Very few people manage it while under direct attack."
  },
  "23": {
    "wiring": "You say the one true sentence that actually matters instead of adding more context, comparison, or reassurance. When someone's been circling the same hurt for an hour, the circling stops almost as soon as you speak. Subtraction is exactly what an overwhelmed person needs, and it's what you supply.",
    "root": "Simplicity got built as trustworthy early because complicated reassurance once failed to land while a plain sentence did. So subtracting became the skill you practice, and elaboration started to feel like a way of avoiding the actual point. Directness earned its place as the reliable move.",
    "defaultMode": "You know the one sentence and offer six instead, cushioned and qualified, and the other person leaves with nothing usable in their hands. Or the essential truth lands at the wrong hour with no warmth around it, arriving as an assault rather than a gift. Timing goes wrong in both directions.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see which version — over-cushioned or too blunt — you're about to deliver until it's already out. The uncertainty about whether your directness is welcome operates below your awareness. It just shows up as mistimed delivery afterward.",
    "underusedStrength": "Your instinct for the essential — saying the one sentence that carries the whole of the situation — consistently reads to people afterward as relief rather than correction. Most people add; you subtract, and subtraction is what overwhelmed people actually need. That instinct is never bluntness in disguise."
  },
  "24": {
    "wiring": "You return to the same feeling until it finally yields something the first three passes didn't. Where others distract themselves out of a difficult emotion, you go back in. That returning produces genuinely original understanding of your own life.",
    "root": "Treating a recurring feeling as unfinished, rather than as a malfunction, got built early as the honest response to something that clearly wasn't done yet. Silencing it the way other people do felt like a false ending. So returning became the method, not a symptom.",
    "defaultMode": "A feeling arrives and, rather than passing through, it gets rerun — the argument replayed, the message reread, the ache pressed once more to check that it's still there. One story gets told for years at the same emotional pitch, and the people who love you begin bracing for it. The quiet on the far side of the feeling never gets reached because you restart just before it arrives.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the difference, in the moment, between a productive fourth pass and a rerun that's going nowhere. Both feel identical from inside — like unfinished business demanding attention. Only the people around you can tell which one it's become.",
    "underusedStrength": "Treating a recurring feeling as unfinished rather than broken produces genuinely original understanding most people never reach, because they abandon a difficult emotion after the first or second pass. Your willingness to sit easily with somebody stuck on a loop, without any urge to hurry them off it, comes directly from this. It's a real capacity, not stubbornness."
  },
  "25": {
    "wiring": "Your heart stays open at the size a situation actually requires when someone brings you something genuinely unbearable. You manage your own discomfort second and attend to them first, in reverse of the usual order. Most people manage their own reaction before they can actually attend to anyone else's.",
    "root": "Staying open through someone else's unbearable feeling got built as the thing that made you trustworthy with the badly hurt. Closing off felt like a betrayal of what they needed from you. So openness under pressure became the standard, even when it cost you something.",
    "defaultMode": "Something wounds you and the aperture narrows — same face, same words, but the openness has quietly gone, and the narrowing gets defended afterward as being sensible now. You turn selectively cold, generous with strangers and quietly armored toward the few people who could actually reach you. The specific closeness you say you want stays just out of range.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the narrowing happen in real time — it feels like reasonable caution, not like closing a door. The people closest to you notice the shift long before you'd ever describe it that way. To you it still looks like being open; to them it's a wall.",
    "underusedStrength": "Holding somebody's pain steadily without needing it tidied or made smaller is rarer than any comforting sentence you could offer. It's why the badly hurt keep finding you. Most people manage their own discomfort first; you don't, and that ordering is the actual gift."
  },
  "26": {
    "wiring": "You find the angle — a joke, a redirection, a well-placed question — that lets people out of tension without anyone losing face. Most people either confront or avoid entirely; your third option is a real skill you use more instinctively than you realize. Hard conversations land quietly around you that would detonate almost anywhere else.",
    "root": "Deflecting tension with a light touch got built as protection — visible need once felt like handing someone an advantage over you. So lightness became the tool that spared other people while also keeping your own state private. The two functions got fused early and never separated.",
    "defaultMode": "Something hurt, and admitting it feels like handing over an advantage, so a version of yourself that was never affected gets presented instead. The same lightness that spares other people becomes a way of never being caught out. The ones closest to you sense they're being handled rather than known.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how the light touch that protects others also protects you from ever being seen fully — the two purposes look identical from inside. It reads as generosity to you. It can read as evasion to the people who want to actually know your real state.",
    "underusedStrength": "Diffusing tension without anyone losing face is a genuine and rare skill, not just a way of avoiding the hard thing. It lets difficult conversations land quietly around you instead of detonating. That's a real service to the people in the room, distinct from just deflecting."
  },
  "27": {
    "wiring": "Your first movement toward someone struggling comes before any calculation about cost or whether they've earned it. Most people run a quick internal check about reciprocity; that check doesn't fire in you. Your care arrives faster and lands cleaner because of it.",
    "root": "Reaching without calculating got built as the honest response, before there was time to run a cost-benefit check on someone in front of you. Hesitating to help felt like it would betray the urgency of what they needed. So the reflex to move first became automatic rather than deliberate.",
    "defaultMode": "Everyone gets fed, and then a quiet score starts running, and you find yourself hurt by people who never knew a debt existed in the first place. In depleted stretches you hoard, refusing small acts that would cost nothing, justified by everything you've already given. Your own generosity starts to feel transactional even in the moments it's entirely real.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the ledger starting — it accumulates quietly under the appearance of ongoing generosity. To you it still feels like giving freely. The tally is invisible until the resentment surfaces and you can't explain where it came from.",
    "underusedStrength": "Moving toward someone struggling without first calculating cost or reciprocity means your care arrives faster and lands cleaner than most people's. That check most people run doesn't fire in you, and the difference is genuinely felt by whoever's on the receiving end. It's a real faculty, not naivety."
  },
  "28": {
    "wiring": "You go all the way into someone's despair with them rather than throwing a rope from the edge. Most people accompany distress up to a point and then start reaching for meaning; you carry no such limit. There's no floor below which you leave.",
    "root": "Going all the way in, rather than stopping at a safe distance, got built as the thing that made your presence actually count. Reaching for meaning too early once felt like abandoning someone mid-collapse. So staying past the point of comfort became the standard you hold.",
    "defaultMode": "A sudden conviction arrives that none of this matters, usually just after a period of intensity has passed. Other people's feelings look small and theatrical, and either you say so out loud or you go missing and return with no explanation. Risk gets chased inside relationships through provoking, gambling, and testing whether anything is real.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the flat, purposeless stretch coming — it arrives as certainty, not as a mood you could question. From inside it feels like clear-eyed truth. To the people around you it looks like a sudden and unexplained withdrawal.",
    "underusedStrength": "Willingness to go the whole way into a feeling, without needing a lesson attached afterward, is why you get trusted with things nobody else in someone's life is ever told. That capacity has no built-in limit, which is rare. Most people's accompaniment has a ceiling; yours doesn't."
  },
  "29": {
    "wiring": "Once you're actually in, your commitment has no reservation inside it and nothing kept back for later use. When somebody needs you through something long and unpleasant, the people around you feel the whole of you arrive at once. Most support gets offered with a private exit built in; yours doesn't.",
    "root": "Total commitment got built as the only version that felt honest — a partial yes once registered as a kind of lie. So a genuine yes became rare and total by design. A reserved yes started to feel worse than an outright no.",
    "defaultMode": "Agreement gets made to be there emotionally, and then you arrive at forty percent, resentful and unreachable, and the other person can't name what's wrong because you technically showed up. A genuine no would end the relationship outright, so you spend yourself on things you never actually wanted. The exhaustion that follows gets blamed on whoever you said yes to.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice, at the moment of saying yes, that it was actually a no you didn't feel able to say. It looks like generosity in the moment. The resentment that follows is the only evidence, and it arrives too late to be useful.",
    "underusedStrength": "Total presence in a chosen moment, with nothing held back for later, changes what another person is willing to risk in your company. You assume everyone means their yes as completely as you do — they don't. That total presence is genuinely rare and worth recognizing in yourself."
  },
  "30": {
    "wiring": "When something moves you — longing, delight, grief — it comes through at full strength rather than getting cooled down first. Most people cool a feeling before letting it out; yours arrives entirely intact. Rooms come alive around you because of it.",
    "root": "Full-strength feeling got built as the only honest version — cooling it down before release once felt like a small betrayal of what was actually happening. So intensity became trustworthy rather than something to manage down. The people who love you learned not to expect a diluted version.",
    "defaultMode": "A longing catches and the entire emotional system organizes around it — the person, the reconciliation, the moment that will finally settle you — and everything else goes thin. The heat gets kept going through drama or pursuit or grievance. People who came for warmth find a furnace instead.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the tipping point where wanting turns from fuel into fixation — it still feels like the same alive intensity you always run on. From outside, the shift is obvious. From inside, it just feels like caring a lot.",
    "underusedStrength": "Feeling arriving at full strength, unmanaged, gives everyone nearby quiet permission to want things again. That intensity isn't a problem to manage down. The people who love you wouldn't trade it for anything calmer or more convenient."
  },
  "31": {
    "wiring": "You say the thing a room has silently agreed never to mention, out loud, and the atmosphere reorganizes around the truth within moments. Most people wait for a permission that never arrives; you simply go first. Going first costs you something real every time.",
    "root": "Speaking the unsaid thing got built as necessary — waiting for consensus once meant the moment closed without anything true being said at all. So going first became the role you occupy, whether or not anyone asked you to. Silence in the face of an obvious tension started to feel like complicity.",
    "defaultMode": "You speak with a certainty that hasn't been earned yet, over people still forming their own feelings, and the whole thing gets called leadership. The position gets committed to hard, and whoever disagrees goes quiet rather than argue, which then reads back to you as agreement. The room stops telling you anything true.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the gap between the confidence in your voice and what you actually know, because the confidence arrives automatically once you've decided to speak first. It feels like certainty from inside. From outside, it can look like overreach.",
    "underusedStrength": "Naming the unspoken tension aloud, first, is why people follow your emotional lead in a crisis — not because you sound certain, but because you said the exposed thing while it still mattered. Almost nobody does that. It costs you something every time, which is exactly why it works."
  },
  "32": {
    "wiring": "You keep an old grief accurately rather than expecting someone to be finished with it by now, even years later. Most sympathy has a shelf life of a few weeks; yours doesn't. That fidelity to what once mattered holds across long stretches of time.",
    "root": "Long-term fidelity to a feeling got built as loyal in a way that short attention wasn't — forgetting what mattered to someone once felt like a small abandonment. So remembering accurately, across years, became the thing you pride yourself on. Moving on too fast started to feel dishonest.",
    "defaultMode": "A relationship starts to matter and something in you calculates the odds, withholds the final twenty percent, and then calls that caution wisdom. Old hurts get preserved as evidence, past wreckage gets replayed in detail, and a defunct disappointment sets the terms of a new person's welcome. Years pass at a safe distance, guarded against a failure that already happened and can't repeat in the same form.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the withheld twenty percent as withholding — it registers as sensible caution instead. The person on the receiving end can feel that something's held back even if they can't name it. You can't feel the gap from your side.",
    "underusedStrength": "Keeping what someone once felt held accurately, without a shelf life, is enormously loyal, especially to anyone carrying something long. Most sympathy fades in a few weeks. Yours doesn't, and that fidelity is one of the more durable things a person can offer another over a lifetime."
  },
  "33": {
    "wiring": "You withdraw after a charged exchange and come back with the whole thing sorted, having found the exact sentence that turned the argument. Most people rerun a conflict on a loop and get nowhere; you set it down, go quiet, and return with the single line worth saying. That gap gives everyone else room to stop performing a reaction they don't actually feel.",
    "root": "Stepping out of the heat before responding got built as necessary — reacting in the moment once produced something you regretted. So the pause became protective, and the retreat became the mechanism for actually digesting what happened. Immediate response started to feel unreliable.",
    "defaultMode": "Someone raises their voice and you go absent behind your own eyes, present in the room and gone from the exchange, so they end up arguing with a wall. Whole conversations vanish afterward, so the same hurt arrives again next month with nothing learned. The other person ends up doing the remembering for both of you.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the moment the useful retreat turns into disappearance — from inside, it still feels like processing. To the other person, you've simply left the conversation. The line between digesting and vanishing is invisible to you in real time.",
    "underusedStrength": "Stepping out of the heat and returning with the one sentence worth saying gives people room to stop performing a reaction they don't actually feel. That habit of digesting before responding is a real skill, not coldness. It changes what the conversation is able to become once you come back."
  },
  "34": {
    "wiring": "What surges in you under pressure is enormous, steady, and calm rather than frantic or scattered. You can stand in front of someone's rage without flinching, which lets them finish saying the thing they've been swallowing for months. Your size stays exactly the same in a confrontation while most people halve themselves.",
    "root": "Staying full-sized under pressure, rather than shrinking, got built as the way you held ground when it mattered. Softening even slightly once got marked down as losing by whoever was watching. So maintaining your full size became something you defend, sometimes past the point it's needed.",
    "defaultMode": "A small slight lands and the response comes out three sizes too big, so the other person goes silent and their silence gets read as agreement. Weeks later they're still handling you carefully, and the conversation that cost them something registered with you as nothing at all. The people who love you learn to time their honesty around your weather.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the disproportion in the moment — the response feels exactly right-sized to you as it's happening. The other person's careful handling of you afterward is the only signal something was off. That signal usually arrives too late to be useful.",
    "underusedStrength": "Staying unfrightened by another person at their worst, and still standing there when they finish, settles a room in a way most people can't manage. It's never struck you as unusual, but it is. That steadiness under someone's rage is a genuine, rare capacity."
  },
  "35": {
    "wiring": "Risk in an emotional room draws you toward it rather than warning you off. You go first into the awkward conversation or the unfinished thing, and your appetite for it makes whoever's with you braver simply by contact. Almost everyone waits until they feel safe before opening a difficult subject; you open it hungry.",
    "root": "Moving toward difficult feeling, rather than away from it, got built as the more alive option — waiting for safety once felt like waiting for a life that never actually arrived. So appetite for the hard conversation became the trustworthy instinct. Caution started to feel like a slow form of not living.",
    "defaultMode": "A settled relationship starts registering as a room with the air removed. Nothing dramatic has happened for a month, so a fight gets started, a message gets sent, an exit gets rehearsed, all in the name of feeling something again. Whoever loves you experiences that as a verdict on them, and is never told it was really about the flatness.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the flatness as the actual trigger — it registers as something wrong with the relationship, not as your own need for intensity. The person you love takes the blame for something that was never about them. That misattribution stays invisible to you at the time.",
    "underusedStrength": "Going first into a difficult conversation, genuinely curious rather than braced, is why people tell you things inside twenty minutes that they've never told anyone else in years. That appetite for emotional weather is real and rare. Most people avoid exactly what you move toward."
  },
  "36": {
    "wiring": "You sit with someone in the middle of the night without trying to fix them, which is a different skill from giving advice and far rarer. You wade into other people's crises without needing the water to settle first. Being near raw human distress without flinching stays uncommon, and the people you've done it for don't forget who was in the room.",
    "root": "Staying present in crisis, without needing to fix it, got built as the more genuine offer — fixing too fast once felt like it dismissed what someone was actually going through. So sitting with, rather than solving, became the skill you trust most. Wanting nothing back in that moment became the standard you hold.",
    "defaultMode": "A settled life starts to feel like something important has gone missing, so a small tension gets pressed on until it becomes an event of the kind you know how to handle. Other people's storms get absorbed as your own weather, and by the end of the week you're carrying several crises with no idea which feelings started out yours. A quiet belief runs underneath that you're only wanted in an emergency.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how much of the crisis you've absorbed as your own until it's already tangled with everything else you're carrying. It doesn't announce itself as borrowed weather. It just feels like your actual mood.",
    "underusedStrength": "Being present at raw human distress without flinching, and without needing it to resolve into a lesson, is uncommon and genuinely valuable. The people you've done it for remember who was in the room. That's a real and specific capacity, not just being unbothered."
  },
  "37": {
    "wiring": "You notice when somebody's being left slightly outside the circle and move toward them before anyone else has registered the gap. Nearly everybody waits to be introduced to a feeling; you offer yours first. That's exactly what makes an exchange safe enough for the other person to enter.",
    "root": "Moving first, offering warmth before it was requested, got built as the thing that kept a group intact. Waiting to be invited in once felt riskier than just including someone yourself. So including people ahead of any signal from them became the reflex.",
    "defaultMode": "Someone raises their voice at the table and you agree with them before you've checked whether you actually do. Months of that build a resentment you'd never say out loud, and it leaks sideways as sulks, small withdrawals, and a martyred kind of helpfulness. Nobody in your circle knows what you actually want, and by now that includes you.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the moment you agree with someone to keep the peace rather than because you actually agree — it happens too fast to catch. It feels, in the moment, like keeping things smooth. The cost only shows up later as resentment you can't source.",
    "underusedStrength": "Moving toward whoever's being left outside the circle, before anyone else notices, is the specific competence that keeps groups of people in one piece. It isn't softness. It's a real skill that most groups depend on without ever naming it."
  },
  "38": {
    "wiring": "You go back for a fourth hard conversation when others gave up at the second. You fight for people who aren't present to defend themselves, without needing them to find out it happened. Things get repaired around you because of that stamina.",
    "root": "Staying in a hard conversation past the point most people quit got built as the way things actually got fixed. Giving up early once meant the real issue never got addressed. So endurance in conflict became a value you hold, sometimes past the point it's useful.",
    "defaultMode": "A partner says something mildly careless and it becomes a two-hour position war neither of you wanted, defended long after you stopped believing in it. Loyalty turns into taking up battles nobody asked you to take, and then feeling wronged when the person defended looks embarrassed rather than grateful. A belief runs underneath that ease has to be earned, and a life without struggle wouldn't count as a life.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice when a fight has become manufactured rather than necessary — it still feels, from inside, like defending something that matters. The people around you can see the fight has outlived its purpose. You're usually the last to see it.",
    "underusedStrength": "Staying in a difficult conversation until something true emerges is genuinely rare, and it's why things get repaired around you that would have stayed broken elsewhere. Most people give up at the second hard exchange. Your stamina for conflict is the thing you discount most about yourself."
  },
  "39": {
    "wiring": "You say the sentence everyone was avoiding, and the whole room breathes out because the tension got named instead of sat inside for another hour. Where others manage an atmosphere by keeping it flat, you push until it moves. Situations that had been stuck for months come unstuck around you.",
    "root": "Provoking honest feeling got built as necessary — a flat room once felt like a room where nothing real was actually happening. So pushing for a reaction became the way you checked whether contact was genuine. Calm silence started to register as distance rather than as peace.",
    "defaultMode": "Somebody gets needled until they finally show you something, and the outburst gets counted as intimacy. A quiet evening starts feeling like distance, and within twenty minutes the sore subject has been found and pressed without a decision to press it. People begin guarding themselves in your company and offering the smooth version, producing exactly the flatness you were trying to break.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the needling as needling while it's happening — it registers as checking whether someone's really there. The other person experiences it as being provoked. That reframe only happens after the fact, if at all.",
    "underusedStrength": "Naming a tension a room has agreed not to mention breaks a stuck situation loose faster than anything else on offer. People are secretly relieved every time you do it. Being the one who breaks the surface is rarer than you think, and it's a genuine service, not just discomfort with quiet."
  },
  "40": {
    "wiring": "The word no leaves you cleanly, with no paragraph of justification attached. You give enormously once you've chosen to, and the giving is wholehearted precisely because it was never extracted from you. You decline what other people find impossible to decline.",
    "root": "A clean no got built as more trustworthy than a resentful yes — saying yes and then quietly resenting it once cost more than the discomfort of refusing outright. So declining cleanly became the skill, and reluctant agreement became the thing you distrust in yourself now. Protecting your own reserves without apology became the standard.",
    "defaultMode": "The refusing arrives too late, after weeks of giving past the line, when there's nothing left to be gracious with. Someone asks for one more small thing and the answer detonates, wildly out of proportion, aimed at entirely the wrong person. Days of unreachable silence follow while the other side tries to work out what they did.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the line being crossed at the time it's crossed — the giving past your limit feels, in the moment, like ordinary generosity. Only the eventual detonation reveals that a limit was quietly exceeded weeks earlier. By then it looks disproportionate to everyone, including you.",
    "underusedStrength": "Declining cleanly, without a paragraph of justification, protects the relationship rather than damaging it. Most people say yes and then resent it for weeks; you decline in the moment and keep things intact. Your yes, when it comes, means something because of it."
  },
  "41": {
    "wiring": "You start emotional weather in a room out of nothing, which is why gatherings tilt toward warmth about ten minutes after you arrive. Most people react to whichever mood is already present; you originate one. Others find themselves inside yours before noticing where it began.",
    "root": "Generating a feeling rather than waiting to catch one got built as necessary because waiting for the right mood to arrive once meant waiting indefinitely. So originating warmth became a skill rather than something that just happens to you. Passivity about mood started to feel like a kind of resignation.",
    "defaultMode": "A conversation gets rehearsed a dozen times before it happens, and when it finally happens you're answering the rehearsal rather than the person in front of you. Disappointment lands on them as coolness, and they have no idea which script they just failed. The actual evening turns up looking thin beside the imagined one.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice when you're responding to your rehearsal instead of the actual person — it feels, from inside, like being prepared. They experience an unexplained flatness or coolness. The mismatch between your internal script and their real presence stays invisible to you.",
    "underusedStrength": "Originating a mood from a standing start, with no cue to work from, is far less common than you assume. Most people simply react to whichever mood is already present. Starting something real in another person from nothing is a genuine and specific ability."
  },
  "42": {
    "wiring": "You sit with grief without hurrying it toward a lesson. Most people get uncomfortable at the exact point where nothing helps; you stay past that point, doing very little and saying less. Being present at a finish without trying to make it mean something steadies the whole room.",
    "root": "Staying through an ending, without rushing it toward meaning, got built as the more honest response — hurrying someone's grief once felt like abandoning them mid-process. So patience with endings became the thing you offer, even when it costs you comfort. Rushing toward closure started to feel like a small dishonesty.",
    "defaultMode": "A relationship dies quietly and gets kept on life support, with the expected version replayed nightly instead of the one that actually happened. Grief gets rushed in other people too, so a friend cries and the silver lining is produced before the crying has properly started. A present moment never gets your full attention because it's crowded with unfinished feeling.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the contradiction between how patiently you sit with others' grief and how quickly you rush your own toward a lesson. It feels consistent from inside. From outside, the double standard is obvious.",
    "underusedStrength": "Accompanying someone all the way to the end of something, including the endings everyone else finds a reason to miss, is a capacity you've never counted as one. Most people get uncomfortable at exactly the point where nothing helps. You stay there, and it steadies the whole room."
  },
  "43": {
    "wiring": "You wait for as long as it takes before answering, and the sentence that eventually arrives reframes the entire thing. Nearly everyone answers at the speed of the provocation; you wait, and what you produce is worth more than five fast responses. You hear what somebody means underneath what they're shouting.",
    "root": "Delaying a response got built as necessary — answering too fast once produced something inaccurate or reactive. So the pause became protective, and slowness in your own timing became trustworthy where speed wasn't. Fast responses started to feel unreliable, even when they were correct.",
    "defaultMode": "Someone brings their distress and appears to be received by nothing, so they say it again louder, at which point you become even less reachable. Advice offered before you're ready gets dismissed outright, including the accurate kind, costing you years of shortcuts people handed over freely. People stop repeating themselves, and you lose the input your insight was built to work on.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how identical your silence looks to shutting down, from the outside, compared to how it feels to you internally as processing. To you it's productive quiet. To them it reads as contempt for what they just said.",
    "underusedStrength": "Waiting past the speed of a provocation and returning with the answer that actually reframes things is a rare tolerance for not knowing yet. That delay is not slowness — it's a genuine capacity held in the middle of somebody else's urgency. The answer that arrives is worth more than five fast ones."
  },
  "44": {
    "wiring": "You know within a minute of meeting someone whether you're safe in their company or not. You read a group's real allegiances long before anyone announces them. Plenty of people need two years and a betrayal to learn what your first handshake told you.",
    "root": "Fast, accurate reads on people got built as protective — waiting to find out the hard way once cost too much. So instant judgment became trustworthy, and slow trust became a risk you weren't willing to take repeatedly. Sorting people early became the way you kept the handful you intended to go the distance with.",
    "defaultMode": "The verdict arrives in seconds and there's no appeal, so a person gets written off over a tone of voice that had nothing to do with you. Someone resembles an old betrayal and inherits the whole sentence, unaware they're serving time for another person's crime. Suspicion runs quietly in the background even of good relationships, and the scanning itself makes a distance the other one can feel.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see when a snap verdict is actually about someone from your past rather than the person in front of you. It feels, in the moment, like an accurate read of them specifically. The mismatch is invisible to you until the relationship has already thinned.",
    "underusedStrength": "Reading a room's real allegiances and a person's safety within minutes, and being accurate about it far more often than not, is a genuine form of loyalty. It's a way of sorting early for the people you intend to go the full distance with. Most people take years to learn what your first read already told you."
  },
  "45": {
    "wiring": "You register who's uncomfortable in a room and adjust the whole evening around them, without announcing it. Where others attend a gathering, you hold one. The difference shows up in whether the quietest person there ends up speaking by the end of the night.",
    "root": "Taking responsibility for a room's emotional temperature got built as necessary early — an unmanaged room once felt genuinely unsafe. So holding the atmosphere became your role, taken on without anyone assigning it to you. Letting the room manage itself started to feel like negligence.",
    "defaultMode": "Somebody's unhappy at your table and it registers as insubordination rather than information, so their mood gets managed instead of heard. Dissent gets absorbed smoothly and generously, and the person still leaves knowing they lost something they can't quite name. A circle forms of people who are loyal and slightly careful, which isn't the same as close.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the shift from responsibility to ownership of the room — it feels, from inside, like the same caretaking the whole way through. The people in the room can tell when they've stopped being allowed to disagree with the mood you're holding. You can't feel that boundary from where you sit.",
    "underusedStrength": "Being answerable for a group's emotional temperature is rarer than charisma and worth considerably more than it, even though nobody hands out credit for it. Where others attend a gathering, you hold one. That difference is a real and specific skill."
  },
  "46": {
    "wiring": "You find the moment when a family in crisis is able to laugh again, and that laugh does more repair than the previous hours managed. You lighten heavy rooms without making anybody's pain smaller. Difficult things become survivable because you carry delight in with you rather than waiting for the serious business to finish first.",
    "root": "Lightness got built as a genuine tool early because a heavy room without any relief once felt unsurvivable. So finding the moment for laughter became a skill, not a deflection. Timing that lightness carefully became something you practiced without naming it.",
    "defaultMode": "A friend starts describing something genuinely bad and a joke is out of your mouth before they finish. It works, which is the problem — the room lifts, the subject moves on, and nobody gets to put the hard thing down properly. Your own grief goes the same way, postponed until it turns up months later wearing an unrelated face.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the moment the joke replaces the hard thing rather than accompanying it — it feels like relief in the moment, for everyone including you. The unfinished conversation is invisible until it resurfaces somewhere else entirely. You rarely connect the two.",
    "underusedStrength": "Finding the moment when a heavy room is able to laugh again does more repair than hours of serious conversation. Most people treat delight as what happens once the serious business is finished; you carry it in with you the whole time. That's a genuine, uncommon skill, not just a personality trait."
  },
  "47": {
    "wiring": "You stay present in a low mood without needing it fixed, and you stay long enough for something to actually change shape. You're unfrightened by other people's darkness, so people bring you their worst weeks unedited. Almost everyone tries to talk somebody out of despair within minutes; you sit inside it with them instead.",
    "root": "Staying inside a hard feeling rather than talking someone out of it got built as the more honest response — rushing someone toward feeling better once felt like dismissing what they were actually going through. So tolerance for sitting in the dark became the skill you trust. Trying to fix it fast started to feel like a small betrayal.",
    "defaultMode": "The descent stops being temporary and becomes an identity, and the low place gets defended as though it were the only truth about you. Comfort arrives and is refused, since accepting it would mean the suffering was optional. The same wound gets rehearsed in the same words in the same year, with none of it moving anywhere.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see when your low mood has stopped being weather and started being a fixed identity — from inside it still feels like being honest about how things are. The people who love you can see the loop has become circular rather than moving. You can't tell the difference from where you're standing.",
    "underusedStrength": "Keeping company with a bad feeling until it turns is genuinely rare — almost everyone tries to talk somebody out of despair within minutes. You sit inside it instead, and that by itself is medicine. You've always assumed everybody could do it; almost nobody can."
  },
  "48": {
    "wiring": "You stay useful in situations with no correct response available, which is exactly where most competent people freeze. Whatever you find to say in the moment lands better than any rehearsed condolence would. You go in empty-handed and something adequate arrives every time.",
    "root": "Improvising rather than relying on a script got built as necessary — waiting for the right prepared words once meant missing the moment entirely. So trusting whatever turns up became the skill, even without being able to explain where it came from. Resourcefulness under pressure became something you rely on more than preparation.",
    "defaultMode": "A voice says you're not enough for this, so you hesitate a beat too long, and that hesitation gets read as indifference. The person turns to someone less careful and more available. People get sent to professionals, books, and other friends — anyone whose adequacy you trust further than your own.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see how often your hesitation, not your actual capability, is what causes someone to look elsewhere. It feels like appropriate caution to you. To them, it reads as you not being available when it mattered.",
    "underusedStrength": "Staying useful in a situation with no correct response, trusting whatever turns up rather than needing a script first, is real resourcefulness under pressure. Most competent people freeze exactly where you improvise. That's a genuine skill, not luck."
  },
  "49": {
    "wiring": "You stop pretending an arrangement is working long before the pretending turns expensive. You know months ahead of most people that a relationship has finished. Your cut comes out clean rather than cruel, a distinction most people never manage.",
    "root": "Ending things cleanly, rather than dragging them out, got built as the more honest option — staying inside a dead arrangement out of politeness once felt like a slower form of dishonesty. So a clean, early ending became the standard you trust. Prolonging something past its actual life started to feel worse than the discomfort of stopping it.",
    "defaultMode": "The ending arrives at the speed of the feeling, before it's checked against anything, and a decade goes over the side in an afternoon. One remark crosses a line and the door shuts permanently, with no route back offered. The cold calm of certainty that follows is the most convincing sensation in the world and is wrong roughly half the time.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the difference, in the moment, between a genuine ending and a reactive one — both arrive with the same cold certainty. The person left outside the door usually never finds out what they did. You can't tell from inside which kind of ending just happened.",
    "underusedStrength": "Knowing months ahead of everyone else that something has genuinely finished, and stopping the pretending before it turns expensive, has saved you more years of your life than you've counted. Plenty of people stay inside dead arrangements for years out of politeness. Your instinct for when something is actually over runs unusually true."
  },
  "50": {
    "wiring": "You feel it immediately when something's gone wrong in a family or a team, long before there's evidence anybody could point at. Nearly everybody notices a broken atmosphere once it's already a crisis; you catch it at the first wrong note. People behave better around you without ever being asked to.",
    "root": "Catching an atmosphere early, before it became a crisis, got built as necessary responsibility — missing the early signal once meant the damage was already done by the time anyone noticed. So early detection became something you carry as a duty, not just a skill. Waiting for confirmation started to feel like negligence.",
    "defaultMode": "The standard hardens into policing, and values that once protected people start being enforced upon them, beginning with the ones who love you most. A relative behaves badly at a wedding and it costs them your warmth for a year, a far heavier sentence than the crime deserved. Every group you enter becomes yours to hold, and nobody offers to take a turn because nobody was ever asked.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the moment protecting a group's standard turns into punishing individuals for falling short of it. It feels, from inside, like consistent principle. The people close to you experience it as a sentence disproportionate to what actually happened.",
    "underusedStrength": "Carrying responsibility for the emotional health of a group is a form of love that almost never gets named as one by the people it protects. You catch a wrong note before it becomes a crisis, while most people only notice once it already is one. That's a genuine and undervalued form of care."
  },
  "51": {
    "wiring": "You act first in an emergency, before fear has finished arriving, which is why people look toward you when the news is bad. Most people freeze for the crucial thirty seconds; you move. That gap is the whole difference between a story people tell afterward and a disaster nobody recovers from.",
    "root": "Moving before fear fully lands got built as protective early — waiting for fear to pass before acting once meant the window closed. So acting fast became the trustworthy response, and hesitation became the thing you distrust in yourself. Speed under shock became a value you hold onto.",
    "defaultMode": "People get woken up whether or not they asked for it, and the shock gets delivered as though it were a favor. A conversation grows too settled and something disruptive gets dropped into it, followed by an odd satisfaction at the scramble that comes next. Everyone nearby ends up living inside your alarms, treating your agitation as a signal about the world rather than weather in you.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see when you've manufactured a jolt rather than responded to a real one — both feel identical in the moment as urgent action. The people around you experience the manufactured version as disruption for its own sake. You genuinely can't tell the difference from inside it.",
    "underusedStrength": "Acting first in a genuine emergency, before fear has finished arriving, is the whole difference between a story people tell afterward and a disaster nobody recovers from. The courage you run on an ordinary day is invisible to you. Being first without being asked is much rarer than you think."
  },
  "52": {
    "wiring": "You hold your reaction in the second it arrives, which gives everyone else in the room time to find their footing again. Pressure that would ricochet through anybody else simply stops when it reaches you. Your presence lowers the temperature of a hard conversation before you've said a word.",
    "root": "Holding a reaction, rather than discharging it immediately, got built as the mature move — expressed feeling once looked dangerous or unattractive in the rooms that mattered to you. So restraint became something you practice as a matter of course. Immediate expression started to feel like a lapse in adulthood.",
    "defaultMode": "Feeling that never got expressed doesn't vanish; it sits there, hardening into something less workable. Someone hurts you and nothing shows on the surface, so they carry on doing it, sincerely unaware anything landed. Then a small thing goes wrong on an ordinary day and four months of held material arrives at once, at the wrong person, in the wrong volume.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the backlog accumulating — each individual instance of holding feeling looks reasonable and small on its own. Only the eventual overflow reveals how much had been stored. By then it looks disproportionate to everyone, including you.",
    "underusedStrength": "Absorbing heat without passing it on has prevented more damage than you've ever counted, and your restraint has stopped being effort and simply become what's true about you. People move toward you in a crisis for exactly that reason. Most people discharge tension immediately onto whoever's nearest; you don't."
  },
  "53": {
    "wiring": "You arrive completely at the start of something, with nothing kept in reserve. Most people hold something back at the start in case it disappoints; you don't. The people you arrive at feel chosen rather than merely met.",
    "root": "Full engagement at the beginning got built as the honest version — holding back in case of disappointment once felt like a smaller, safer version of actually showing up. So arriving completely became the standard, even when the middle stretch asked for something different. Reserve at the start started to feel like a kind of dishonesty.",
    "defaultMode": "The person who felt chosen last month is now working out what changed, because enthusiasm made promises your attention had no intention of keeping. Feedback arrives and lands as an attack rather than information, since the excitement it interrupts feels like the truest thing about you. A trail forms of people who loved the first three weeks and never got the fourth.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the drop from full-volume beginning to quieter middle as a drop at all — from inside, your attention just moved somewhere else naturally. The person on the other end experiences it as a withdrawal they can't explain. You genuinely don't register that anything changed.",
    "underusedStrength": "Arriving at full emotional volume, with nothing kept in reserve, is a resource others borrow from constantly without noticing where the extra energy in the room came from. Most people hold something back at the start in case it disappoints. Your beginnings carry a whole group through its awkward first weeks."
  },
  "54": {
    "wiring": "You feel the pull toward more life, more depth, more contact, and that pull drags whoever's nearby upward too. Where most people learn to want quietly and settle early, your appetite stays intact. Your life keeps enlarging while others plateau.",
    "root": "Open wanting got built as fuel rather than embarrassment — muting your appetite once felt like accepting a smaller life than the one available to you. So staying openly hungry for more became the trustworthy instinct. Settling for less started to feel like the actual danger.",
    "defaultMode": "Whatever somebody gives you gets measured against what was expected rather than received as it is. A friend does well and something sour arrives ahead of the congratulations, uninvited and quickly hidden. Every arrival feels thin, so the climbing resumes before you've felt anything about the place you just reached.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the moment you've stopped receiving an arrival and started scoring it against expectation instead. It feels, from inside, like ordinary ambition. The people around you can see you skip past things you actually achieved without pausing to feel them.",
    "underusedStrength": "Open, unembarrassed yearning for more life is an engine, not a flaw — it's the exact fuel that gets people out of situations they were told to accept. That refusal to be satisfied drags whoever's nearby upward too, whether or not they asked for it. Most people learn to want quietly; you never fully did."
  },
  "55": {
    "wiring": "You know a low will pass without anybody having to talk you out of it. You keep your feelings and work them through on your own rather than handing them to whoever's nearest. Your relationships stay clean of debts nobody agreed to.",
    "root": "Owning your own mood, rather than handing it to someone else, got built as the responsible thing to do — offloading a feeling onto whoever's closest once felt like it cost them something they hadn't agreed to. So keeping your own weather became the standard you hold. Expecting someone else to manage your mood started to feel unfair.",
    "defaultMode": "The mood lands and immediately looks for a culprit, usually whoever happened to be standing in the kitchen at the time. Someone answers a message slowly and the whole afternoon goes into building a case against them, complete with evidence and back catalogue. Feelings get treated as verdicts about other people rather than weather passing through.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the moment your low mood turns into a case against someone else — it feels, in the moment, like legitimate grievance. The other person had nothing to do with the actual cause. That mismatch is invisible to you until well after the fact.",
    "underusedStrength": "Riding out your own emotional weather without handing it to whoever's nearest is genuinely freeing and far less common than you've assumed. Nearly everyone hands their feelings to whoever's closest; you keep yours and work it through. Owning your own weather is the most unusual thing about you."
  },
  "56": {
    "wiring": "You're entirely inside a good moment rather than half elsewhere while it's happening. Most people are half-present while something good is going on; you're not, and the difference is registered by everyone at the table. You turn an ordinary evening into something people describe a decade later.",
    "root": "Full presence in pleasure got built as the point — being half elsewhere during a good moment once felt like wasting the one thing worth actually being present for. So total attention to enjoyment became the standard. Distraction during a good moment started to feel like a small loss.",
    "defaultMode": "The moment a feeling levels off, something brighter gets located, and the current room quietly loses you. A conversation goes deep and slow and your hand reaches for a phone, a story, a drink — anything with more color in it than this. People notice being left mid-sentence, and after a while they stop offering the slow material where the real closeness lives.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the reach for stimulation as a reach — it feels like natural curiosity, not like leaving. The person you're mid-conversation with experiences it as being abandoned. You genuinely don't register the exit as an exit.",
    "underusedStrength": "Full-blooded enjoyment, given without holding back, is handed out constantly and almost never counted by you as one of your abilities. Most people are half elsewhere while something good is happening; you're entirely inside it. That capacity is what turns an ordinary evening into something people remember for years."
  },
  "57": {
    "wiring": "You know when somebody's not alright while they're still saying they're fine, and you adjust toward them without making a scene. You detect a change in a room before anything visible has happened. Children and animals settle around you long before the adults do.",
    "root": "Reading the atmosphere before it became explicit got built as necessary early — waiting for someone to say it outright once meant missing the actual moment. So trusting the shift under the words became reliable in a way words alone weren't. Early detection of a change became the sense you lean on.",
    "defaultMode": "Ordinary rooms fill with warnings and dread becomes impossible to tell apart from information. A partner comes home quiet and within a minute a catastrophe has been constructed and acted upon, manufacturing the very tension you were sensing the absence of. Certainty about what somebody feels gets ahead of asking, and people find themselves accused of moods they were never in.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the moment a real signal turns into a manufactured worry — both arrive with the same physical urgency. From inside, both feel like accurate perception. The people around you can tell when you've built a story ahead of any actual evidence.",
    "underusedStrength": "Detecting a shift in a room before anything visible has happened is fine-grained perception, not anxiety, and it's been discounted as nerves for most of your adult life. Where others need words before responding to distress, you answer the atmosphere itself. That early warning is a genuine asset."
  },
  "58": {
    "wiring": "You feel exactly where a situation is dragging and put energy at that point, which is why stuck moods lift once you arrive. Most people wait for their own mood to improve before generating any; you make yours and hand it out. Somebody else's good week can come from twenty minutes with you and no advice at all.",
    "root": "Generating good energy rather than waiting for it got built as necessary — passivity about mood once meant waiting indefinitely for something to shift. So actively creating aliveness in a room became the skill, rather than something that just happened to you. Waiting to feel better before offering anything started to feel wasteful.",
    "defaultMode": "The same sensitivity that finds joy finds fault, and whatever's wrong with a room announces itself before anything good gets noticed. A partner's small habit becomes unbearable on an ordinary day, and the improvement you suggest lands on them as a verdict rather than an idea. A long project of correcting the people nearest you forms, none of whom applied for the work.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice how often 'helping' has slid into correcting someone who didn't ask to be improved. It feels like generosity from where you sit. To them, it can feel like being treated as a project.",
    "underusedStrength": "Generating aliveness in a room rather than waiting for your own mood to improve first is a completely different economy from most people's. Someone's good week can come from twenty minutes with you and no advice whatsoever. That's a real skill, not just an outgoing personality."
  },
  "59": {
    "wiring": "You go straight at the real subject, so a friendship with you reaches in a year or two a depth most friendships take a decade to approach. You confide first, which gives other people permission to stop performing a version of themselves for you. Almost everybody manages closeness by keeping a few small untruths in circulation; you clear them out.",
    "root": "Directness about your own real feelings got built as the shortcut to actual closeness — small polite untruths once felt like they were costing more than they saved. So confiding first, plainly, became the move that reliably worked. Managing distance through politeness started to feel like the slower, worse option.",
    "defaultMode": "A defensible half-answer comes out automatically when someone asks how you are, before any decision was made about what to say. Closeness gets performed with the full technique of the real thing, which fools everybody including you, and leaves you privately unmet. Precisely the thing you wanted — somebody knowing you and staying anyway — stays out of reach.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the automatic half-answer as an edit — it comes out fast enough to feel like the actual truth in the moment. Only afterward, if at all, do you notice something got smoothed over. The gap between what you said and what was true is invisible to you as it happens.",
    "underusedStrength": "Confiding first gives other people direct permission to stop performing a version of themselves for you. Your appetite for being genuinely known, including the parts of you that aren't flattering, is braver than you've ever given yourself credit for. That directness is what lets a friendship reach real depth fast."
  },
  "60": {
    "wiring": "You name the constraint plainly when the news is bad, and the room gets its feet back because of it. Most people meet hard facts with a burst of optimism that helps nobody; you don't inflate the situation. People trust you in a crisis because you've never once sold them comfort they couldn't use.",
    "root": "Plain accuracy about a hard limit got built as more respectful than false reassurance — inflated optimism once failed people at the exact moment they needed the truth. So naming the constraint became the kinder move, even when it wasn't the easy one. Refusing to oversell hope became a standard you hold.",
    "defaultMode": "Somebody's fragile new plan gets met with every reason it won't work. The accuracy isn't the problem — the timing is, arriving while they're still gathering courage to try, which is when it does maximum damage. Your own feeling gets rationed on the same principle, so joy gets inspected for exaggeration before it's allowed near the surface.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't notice the difference between accurate and cruelly-timed until the damage is already done. It feels, in the moment, like being honest. The other person experiences the timing as being deflated right when they needed encouragement.",
    "underusedStrength": "Refusing to inflate a situation is a form of respect, and it's why people trust you in a crisis. The plain sentence you offer is kinder than the reassurance everyone else is producing. Most people meet hard facts with empty optimism; you name the actual constraint and the room gets its feet back because of it."
  },
  "61": {
    "wiring": "You go quiet in the presence of something enormous, and that stillness changes the register for everyone standing nearby. Awe, dread, and love arrive in you at full volume without breaking anything. You stay exactly where you are while most people flinch away and reach for a joke.",
    "root": "Staying still in the presence of something enormous, rather than deflecting it, got built as the more honest response — flinching away from a huge feeling once felt like a small betrayal of how real it was. So tolerance for feeling far too much became a capacity you built rather than avoided. Deflection started to feel like a lesser response to something that deserved better.",
    "defaultMode": "Certainty arrives dressed as revelation, which is the most dangerous feeling you own. Something enormous lands and gets taken as a message meant specifically for you, then acted upon in a way that costs a relationship. Ordinary reassurance becomes impossible to accept, since whoever offers it clearly has no idea what you're carrying.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the moment genuine feeling tips into a private, unchecked certainty about what it means. It feels, from inside, exactly like insight. The isolation that follows is the only sign that the certainty went further than the evidence supported.",
    "underusedStrength": "Bearing feeling at a size other people ration carefully is capacity, not instability, and hardly anybody in your life actually has it. Your tolerance for the overwhelming, without needing to joke it away, is why what comes through you afterward is worth the wait. Most people flinch from what you can hold steady."
  },
  "62": {
    "wiring": "You separate three tangled emotions inside one conversation and hand them back in order and named. You find the exact word for what somebody's going through, and the accuracy of it visibly relieves them on the spot. People bring you their confusion before anybody else hears it.",
    "root": "Precision about feeling got built as the more useful form of sympathy — general comfort once left someone still confused about what they were actually experiencing. So getting the detail exactly right became the skill you offer, more generously to others than to yourself. Vague sympathy started to feel like it wasn't actually helping anyone.",
    "defaultMode": "A description of the emotion gets offered where the emotion itself was wanted. Someone is crying and receives a clarification about what they're actually experiencing, technically correct and completely beside the point. Small inaccuracies in how others describe their own lives get corrected by reflex, and each correction spends a little of the trust that made them speak.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the moment precision replaces actual presence — from inside, naming the feeling accurately still feels like connecting. The other person can feel the difference between being understood and being analyzed. You often can't.",
    "underusedStrength": "Accuracy about inner states is a real skill, and being understood precisely comforts far more than being understood broadly. You spend it on other people far more generously than you've ever spent it on yourself. Where others offer sympathy in general terms, you get the detail right."
  },
  "63": {
    "wiring": "You ask the thing in the room that nobody wanted asked, and the honesty that follows is worth ten seconds of general discomfort. Almost everyone accepts an explanation because it settles the atmosphere; you keep going until the answer is actually true. You question comfort that arrives too easily.",
    "root": "Refusing to be soothed by something false got built as loyalty, not suspicion — accepting a comfortable but untrue explanation once cost more later than the discomfort of questioning it would have. So pressing past easy comfort became the standard you hold, even when it's unwelcome. Settling for atmosphere over accuracy started to feel like a small betrayal of the group.",
    "defaultMode": "Love arriving gets examined for motive rather than received, and the examination is felt by whoever offered it. A fast internal audit begins, asking what they want, what they've missed, how long before they revise it. Tests get set that nobody's ever told about, and when the person fails one, the doubt reports back that it was right all along.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the audit running in real time — it feels like ordinary discernment, not like testing someone who hasn't consented to being tested. The person on the receiving end experiences it as being kept at arm's length. You can't feel the distance from your own side of it.",
    "underusedStrength": "Refusing to be soothed by something false is a rare form of loyalty, and the people who understand that trust you completely. Almost everyone accepts an explanation because it settles the atmosphere; you keep going until it's actually true. That protects the whole group from a comfort that would have cost them later."
  },
  "64": {
    "wiring": "You hold six unsorted feelings at once without forcing them into a tidy story before their time. You let a mess stay a mess until the picture assembles itself. What surfaces beats every quick conclusion that was available at the start.",
    "root": "Tolerating unresolved feeling got built as more honest than premature conclusions — grabbing the first available explanation once produced something that turned out to be wrong. So patience with confusion became trustworthy, and quick certainty became something you distrust in yourself. Waiting for the real picture became the standard.",
    "defaultMode": "The swirl arrives all at once and something has to be done about it immediately, so a decision gets made purely to stop the noise. A relationship ends, a message is sent, an apology is offered for a mood you haven't understood yet, and none of it fits what was actually happening. Decisions made under that pressure don't make sense in hindsight.",
    "blindSpot": "You don't see the difference, in the moment, between patient tolerance of confusion and a decision made just to end it. Both can look like the same unresolved state from inside. Only the outcome — a decision that doesn't hold up — reveals which one actually happened.",
    "underusedStrength": "Tolerating emotional confusion better than almost anyone, and letting a mess stay a mess until it resolves on its own timeline, is a strength most people mistake for slowness. Most people grab the first available explanation to end the discomfort; you wait it out. The understanding you eventually reach is worth the delay."
  }
});
